00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.550 align:start position:0%
 
hey everyone this is kevin from  thechesswebsite.com and today we're  going to continue our series  on in-game tactics now we'll let you  know that the last video did not quite  get the number of  views that i was hoping for so not sure  if that means  uh that everyone's just not as  interested in the end games but i am  going to try something  different and that is to make shorter  videos of  in games still have a lot of videos that  i'd like to make on endgames but let me  know if you  like the shorter condensed versions  of these i want to make sure that you  understand that the concepts but think i  can get through them  very quickly so without further ado  we'll go ahead and jump into it  one thing that i see a lot in chess  games  people send me their games all the time  to review  is when they approach the in-game  they're many times not sure  the best way to approach a king and pond  in game now sometimes they have a king  and a pawn versus to king  i've done another video on that i will  do others as well  but sometimes it's it's just like this  it's both sides have equal material  and they don't know how to approach in  this position right here  one thing that white many times will try  and it's white's move  is to race as fast as possible over to  this pawn on a7 capture it  thinking that okay the king and the pawn  are going to be here white's going to be  up in material  but unfortunately that's not going to  work and so let's take a look at  why that does not work and then talk  about some of the reasons  that it doesn't and then figure out the  best line that white can take to make  sure that they  win in this position  so white has three options can come to  e8  e7 or e6 e7 and e8 are going to work  very similar so we'll take a look at  that  e7 and you could see black coming here  to  c3 king 2 d6 doesn't really matter d6 or  d7  white is still just approaching this a7  square  and then king here to d4  now this is a an important square and  we'll take a look at this later in  the video but d4 and d5  are critical squares as well as c7 this  will make more sense as we get later  into the video now from white standpoint  they can't really go south so they're  gonna be  they can't go to c5 d5 or  e5 so they're just going to continue  with their same plan as before and  that's to come over to  this pawn here on a7 so they're going to  march that way king's going to come to  e5  king 2 b7 king to d6  and after king to a7 then king c7 as we  said before this is a critical square  if black can get his king to c7 this is  going to be  a drawn game now the king can't come to  b8  the king is pretty much stuck so let's  say it comes to  a8 well then now just king to c8 still  able to get to c7 fairly easily and then  after a7 then king to  c7 and this is stalemates  white cannot move at all no one's under  check so this is a drawn game so it  looked like  white could just race over take that  black pawn  but that did not work out so let's go  back to the very beginning and start to  take a look at  where white went wrong and how white  could have continued  to actually win the game  now earlier i mentioned that these  squares in the center  this d4 and d5 are critical squares and  eventually white wants to stop  the black king from coming here to c7  so you always want to calculate where  your opponent can go  and then stop them from getting there  before you do anything else make sure  that you stop them from executing the  game plan that they have  white knows that they're going to be  able to reach the pawns  faster than the black king right here  because it's white's  move but they need to make sure they go  about it the right way and since the  squares in the center here are critical  then white needs to start with the move  king 2  e6 now black's going to same plane as  before needs to come to the center  king 2 c3 but then after  king 2 d5 you can see that black cannot  move  to the d4 square this exactly where  black wanted to go can't come to  c4 as well so if we look at the next  move for  black probably just going to come to b4  makes sense you don't want to get  further away from the action here king  to  c6 and maybe king to c4 because it's  again blocking off some of the squares  that the black king  can go to so if we continue king to  b7 king up here to c5  but after the king captures on a7 you  can see that  in the earlier example the king was able  to come here to  c7 secure this square it's not able to  do that it tries  it's coming here to c6 but now i can  play the move king  to b8 and now  the black king cannot come to c7  and that is the main difference in white  stopping the king  from coming to this d4 square early on  and so now doesn't really matter where  the black king goes let's say it comes  here to b6  well now you can see pawn to a7  and then the next move it's going to  push forward to a8 and it's going to  promote to a queen so very very  important  that white starts with the move e6 and  if it doesn't  let's say you're playing as black  you need to understand that if they make  a mistake and play king to e7  that you shouldn't try to race them you  shouldn't play king 2  b3 because they could just play king 2  d7 and even if you try to go to the  center of the board if you play  king 2 c4 now well you're just behind  in a move they can play king 2 c6  and this is stopping a lot of the  squares that you'd want to go to whether  that's c5 or  d5 and so they're really just forcing  you to play king 2 d4 but you've reached  the square  one move slower than you would have  before so now they can play  king 2 b7 and now you're chasing them  king 2  c5 king 2 a7  but then you can see king 2 c6 are now  king to b8 and they're stopping that  square here on  c7 so because you made that one critical  error of king to b3 early on  you're not able to reach the squares  that you needed to so this is  an in-game tactic always be thinking  about how your opponent can stop you  from winning even if you calculate yes i  can get to the pond quicker  see how you can make sure to stop your  opponent from  stopping you so i have a lot more of  these videos but if you wouldn't mind  feel free hitting the like button  let me know if there's other types of  videos you'd like me to see in the end  game do have some king and pawn  in games whether that's just more king  or pawns on one side  i do think king and pawn is something  that everyone  should focus more on but definitely  interested to hear what everyone  has to say thank you so much for  watching i'll see you guys in the next  video  you

